SETS: A LANGUAGE FOR BELONGING

A set is a collection of distinct objects (elements).
Membership is the only primitive: everything else is defined from it.

FUNDAMENTALS
  element / member     a thing that belongs to a set
  set                  unordered collection of distinct elements
  empty set            the unique set with no elements
  universe / domain    the collection of all objects under consideration
  cardinality          number of elements in a set

BASIC RELATIONS
  a ∈ A                a is an element of A          (membership)
  a ∉ A                a is not an element of A      (non-membership)
  A ⊆ B                every element of A is in B    (subset)
  A ⊂ B or A ⊊ B       A ⊆ B and A ≠ B               (proper subset)
  A ⊇ B                B ⊆ A                         (superset)
  A ⊃ B or A ⊋ B       B ⊂ A                         (proper superset)
  A = B                A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A               (equality)
  A ≠ B                not (A = B)

OPERATIONS
  ∅                    empty set
  A ∩ B                intersection: elements in both A and B
  A ∪ B                union: elements in A or B or both
  A \ B or A − B       difference / relative complement: in A but not B
  A △ B or A ⊖ B       symmetric difference: in exactly one of A or B
  Aᶜ or A′             complement: elements of the universe not in A
  P(A) or 2^A          power set: set of all subsets of A
  |A|                  cardinality of A
  (a, b)               ordered pair
  A × B                Cartesian product: set of all ordered pairs (a, b)

COMMON NUMBER SETS
  ℕ or N               natural numbers
  ℤ or Z               integers
  ℚ or Q               rational numbers
  ℝ or R               real numbers
  ℂ or C               complex numbers
  𝕌 or U               universal set (context-dependent)

NOTES
  Sets contain no duplicates and order does not matter: {1, 2} = {2, 1}.
  The empty set is a subset of every set: ∅ ⊆ A for any A.
  Power set of a finite set with n elements has 2^n elements.
  All operations above are defined solely in terms of membership.

RELATIONAL DYNAMIC GEOMETRY OF SETS

A set is a region. Membership is incidence. Operations are motions that change which points stand in relation.

PRIMITIVES
  point / element      an atomic locus
  region / set         a collection of loci held in mutual relation
  incidence            the basic relation: a point lies in a region
  ambient space        the total locus field under consideration
  configuration        the current arrangement of all incidences

INCIDENCE RELATIONS
  a ∈ A                point a is incident to region A
  a ∉ A                point a lies outside region A
  A ⊆ B                every point incident to A is incident to B
                       (A is contained in B)
  A ⊂ B or A ⊊ B       containment with at least one point of B outside A
  A = B                identical incidence patterns
  boundary shift       change in which points are incident after an operation

DYNAMIC OPERATIONS
  ∅                    null region (no points incident)
  A ∩ B                overlap: points incident to both regions simultaneously
  A ∪ B                merger: points incident to either region
  A \ B                excision: points of A released from incidence with B
  A △ B                exclusive zones: points incident to exactly one region
  Aᶜ                   exterior: points of the ambient space not incident to A
  P(A)                 configuration space of A: all possible subregions
                       obtainable by selecting subsets of its points
  |A|                  measure of the region (count of incident points)
  A × B                product space: paired loci (a from A, b from B)

GEOMETRIC READING
  containment          nested regions
  overlap              intersecting regions
  excision             cutting one region by another
  complement           the surrounding field relative to a region
  power set            the lattice of all possible sub-configurations
  equality             coincidence of boundaries and interiors

DYNAMICS
  Every operation is a map from incidence patterns to new incidence patterns.
  The only invariant is the underlying point set; relations are rewritten.
  Subset chains are inclusion paths through the configuration lattice.
  The empty region is fixed under every operation that removes incidence.
  Finite regions generate a finite lattice of 2^|A| subregions.
